The old net node — functional fragment of an old AI giving access to something else:

The fragment isn't the destination. It's a key. The AI fragment has been sitting in the buried node for decades, partially functional, and it's been quietly maintaining access to something that was never supposed to survive the collapse. What that something is — the other side of the coin — is what makes controlling the node matter beyond just the archive records.

The fragment recognizes specific neural signatures. Not just anyone who finds the node can use it. It was designed to respond to something specific — possibly a lineage of users, possibly a specific augmentation type, possibly something connected to the Surge's original architecture. That last option is interesting — if the Surge was built using components that share architecture with what the old AI fragment recognizes, then Kai's presence near the node would trigger something nobody anticipated. He wasn't looking for it. He didn't know it existed. But something in him opens a door.

That's not a coincidence in the story. That's evidence that the Surge's installation wasn't random. Someone put it in him knowing what it would eventually unlock.

